  "narrative": "The 1970s was a transformative time for Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture. As part of the broader natural hair movement, which originated in the 1960s, women began to reject chemical treatments and instead celebrate their afro-textured hair. The Afro itself became a symbol of black identity and pride, with its signature rounded shape and curly pattern. Cornrows, another traditional African hairstyle, were also gaining popularity as a way to showcase one's cultural heritage. Protective hairstyles like Bantu knots and box braids emerged as a response to the environmental stressors that often damaged afro-textured hair. While documentation from this era is limited, it's clear that women of color were experimenting with new styles and embracing their natural textures as a form of self-expression and resistance against Eurocentric beauty standards.",
